      "math_academy_id": "q-50865",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{4n}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=0$ of ${f}^{′}(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{4n}=\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}+⋯.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+⋯.\n$$",
